  "verdict": "D1 CONFIRMED as a header-text defect on one side and a defensive implementation on the other. Executed on linux-x86_64: pre-guard failures (a) leave *out_handle at the caller's sentinel and return 100 (5/5 cases); every post-guard failure (c) leaves NULL: 104 UNSUPPORTED_CONTAINER, 103 IO, 112 OUT_OF_MEMORY (calloc injected via -Wl,--wrap=calloc). A caller holding a live prior handle in that slot loses the only reference while the handle stays fully usable (song_probe on the saved copy: status 0, 44100 Hz, 2 ch). Path (b) is structurally empty: songcore_ffmpeg.c:823-824 is a returning guard and 825 is the deposit, with no statement between them. Compiled artifacts on BOTH release targets contain the post-guard NULL store (linux 221c 'movq $0x0,(%rsi)'; mingw 2100 'movq $0x0,(%rdx)'), so changing the implementation would change machine code on both targets, while re-wording the header changes no code at all. No in-repo production consumer depends on either reading.",
